our edges for two lighter ones remains. The first vertex of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tour"},{"text":" stays first. Only ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tour.vertices"},{"text":" is read: the result uses the lightest edge of each","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pair, as every tour does. nil below three vertices.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A move’s gain is formed as (d(a, b) + d(c, e)) − (d(a, c) + d(b, e)), and the scan stops","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"when no move gains anything. With floating-point weights, rounding decides which moves","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"gain.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"aside","style":"note","name":"Precondition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The graph is complete; "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tour.vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" lists every vertex of the graph"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"exactly once; no weight is NaN or infinite; the sum of any two weights fits in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"W"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"type":"aside","style":"note","name":"Complexity","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"O(n²) per improving move."}]}]}]}],"abstract":[{"code":"tour","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" improved by 2-opt to a 2-optimal tour.","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/Tours\/documentation\/Tours\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/twoOptTour(improving:weight:)"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Tours\/documentation\/Tours","doc:\/\/Tours\/documentation\/Tours\/GraphProtocols","doc:\/\/Tours\/documentation\/Tours\/GraphProtocols\/Graph"]]},"sections":[],"references":{"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/Graph/Edges-swift.associatedtype":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"associatedtype"},{"kind":"text","t